            <ArticleTitle>A large solitary fibroma of the deep compartment of the sole.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0958-2592(16)30055-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.foot.2016.12.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 50-year-old lady presented with a right foot mass and pain for 1year. Magnetic resonance imaging showed a 3.9×3.2×5cm mass on plantar side of the forefoot deep to the major flexor tendons and plantar aponeurosis and inferior to 1st-4th metatarsals. The mass extended dorsally through the intermetatarsal space to the foot dorsum. Ultrasound guided biopsy was performed, which confirmed it was a fibroma. This case was further complicated by pathological fracture of the 3rd metatarsal. It was resected through a dorsal incision and surgical dislocation of the 2nd and 3rd tarsometatarsal joints. Magnetic resonance imaging was repeated 1year later and showed complete resection of the lesion without recurrence.</AbstractText>
